<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Contamination fears with washing and avoidance rituals taking about four hours a day in a 41-year-old aviator and businessman, ten months after a near-fatal plane crash, who has chronic pain and an opioid use disorder on prescribed codeine. Please confirm the diagnosis and advise on medication, exposure and response prevention, and the part his household staff play in his rituals. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within one week, on the same day as his one-week primary care visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this obsessive-compulsive disorder, and what is the insight specifier? Please consider obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, illness anxiety disorder, post-traumatic stress after the crash and a psychotic disorder.</item><item>Which SSRI and what target dose do you advise alongside buprenorphine?</item><item>How should exposure and response prevention be set up for a man with a demanding schedule and strong wish for privacy?</item><item>How should his household staff be involved, given that they carry out many of his rituals?</item><item>What should we do if the response is poor?</item><item>Do you agree with the safety plan?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Hughes, a 41-year-old aviator and aircraft builder, is seen at the request of Dr. Garrity-Moyo one week after intake. He describes a fear for his health since boyhood and, for about a year, fears of contamination that became much worse after the crash ten months ago: unwanted thoughts that he will be contaminated from outside, by people, door handles, money and food, with washing, cleaning and avoidance that take about four hours a day. He knows the fears are out of proportion but cannot resist the rituals. He does not feel that things must be done in a perfect order for their own sake, he has no fixed belief that he has a serious illness, and he has no nightmares or flashbacks of the crash, though he avoids talking about it. He has no hallucinations and no beliefs that others are harming him. His staff wash before handing him anything, pass him objects wrapped in tissue and screen visitors for illness. He wants treatment and wants it kept private. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Howard Robard Hughes Jr. was born in Houston, Texas, on December 24, 1905, the only child of an oilman whose drill bit became the foundation of the family fortune. [D]</item><item>After an unexplained paralysis in 1919, as a boy, he developed what a state historical handbook calls a lifetime phobic regard for his health; a psychologist who studied his life after his death concluded that his fear for his health began in childhood. [D]</item><item>He suffered a nervous breakdown in 1944. [D]</item><item>On July 7, 1946, on the first flight of the XF-11 reconnaissance plane, he crashed into houses in Beverly Hills and was critically injured, with a crushed chest, a collapsed lung, multiple broken bones and second- and third-degree burns; he nearly died. [D]</item><item>Drugs for pain were first given to him after the 1946 crash; the crash left him with lingering pain and an addiction to codeine, the beginning of a lifelong dependence on painkillers. [D]</item><item>His fear of germs grew throughout his life, with efforts to protect himself from contamination from outside; a news magazine linked his celebrated fear of germs in part to the 1946 crash, which injured his lungs and left him susceptible to bronchial infection. [D]</item></list><paragraph>9 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

